1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Thursday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:09,720
A script calls the API of the platform with Aziz's phone number, and tries passwords from a leaked list, one after another.

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:11,184
Aziz is asleep.

4
00:00:11,234 --> 00:00:13,344
His password is in the platform.

5
00:00:13,394 --> 00:00:19,568
So how does a platform keep a password that it must check, but can never read?

6
00:00:19,618 --> 00:00:24,728
On Sunday, Aziz typed that password two times, and became a customer.

7
00:00:24,778 --> 00:00:28,712
On Monday at the café, his login began with it, before any code.

8
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:32,624
Behind that first step is a box that holds the password itself.

9
00:00:32,674 --> 00:00:34,496
First, where it lives.

10
00:00:34,546 --> 00:00:38,192
Aziz has one phone number, and the number has one password.

11
00:00:38,242 --> 00:00:42,704
So the platform keeps exactly one row for that number, in Postgres.

12
00:00:42,754 --> 00:00:46,256
The row belongs to the customer gateway, not to Keycloak.

13
00:00:46,306 --> 00:00:48,584
Keycloak never sees the password at all.

14
00:00:48,634 --> 00:00:50,456
Why not let Keycloak keep it?

15
00:00:50,506 --> 00:00:54,320
Because the gateway must check the password before it sends any code.

16
00:00:54,370 --> 00:01:01,040
If Keycloak held it, the code would go out first, and the password would travel to Keycloak at every login.

17
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:02,912
Now, what is in the row.

18
00:01:02,962 --> 00:01:04,784
Not the password.

19
00:01:04,834 --> 00:01:10,160
The gateway runs the password through Argon2id, a memory-hard hashing function.

20
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:15,224
Memory-hard means that every single check needs a real block of memory and real time.

21
00:01:15,274 --> 00:01:18,152
For the gateway, that is one check per login.

22
00:01:18,202 --> 00:01:27,128
For an attacker with a stolen copy of the table, every guess costs the same, so millions of guesses become very slow and very expensive.

23
00:01:27,178 --> 00:01:33,296
Before the hashing, the gateway adds a salt: random bytes, new for each password.

24
00:01:33,346 --> 00:01:40,016
So two customers with the same password still get two different digests, and no table of ready answers works.

25
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:46,112
The row keeps one string: the name of the algorithm, its settings, the salt and the digest.

26
00:01:46,162 --> 00:01:50,168
The settings come from the configuration of the installation, not from the code.

27
00:01:50,218 --> 00:01:56,264
The platform keeps no older password, and only the login check of the gateway may read the row.

28
00:01:56,314 --> 00:02:00,752
Older functions like bcrypt were not chosen, because they are not memory-hard.

29
00:02:00,802 --> 00:02:02,624
Back to Thursday night.

30
00:02:02,674 --> 00:02:06,440
Every login starts with the password, before any code goes out.

31
00:02:06,490 --> 00:02:09,704
The gateway compares the digests in constant time.

32
00:02:09,754 --> 00:02:11,984
And here is a detail worth copying.

33
00:02:12,034 --> 00:02:18,080
When the number belongs to nobody, the gateway still runs a check, against a fixed fake digest.

34
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:22,808
The answer is the same, wrong number or password, and it takes the same time.

35
00:02:22,858 --> 00:02:26,288
So the script cannot even learn whether a customer exists.

36
00:02:26,338 --> 00:02:30,968
Each wrong password adds one to a counter in Valkey, for this number.

37
00:02:31,018 --> 00:02:35,792
The gateway also writes an audit event and tells the bank's antifraud system.

38
00:02:35,842 --> 00:02:40,952
At the configured limit, the gateway sets a lock in Valkey, for the configured time.

39
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:43,592
Now password login for this number is locked.

40
00:02:43,642 --> 00:02:47,840
Aziz gets an SMS and a push: password login locked.

41
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:52,328
The script gets one answer: too many wrong passwords, try again later.

42
00:02:52,378 --> 00:02:55,664
When the time is over, the lock ends by itself.

43
00:02:55,714 --> 00:03:00,320
On Friday morning, Aziz reads the message and opens the mobile bank.

44
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:02,192
It works.

45
00:03:02,242 --> 00:03:04,064
A lock is not a block.

46
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:08,912
His session keeps running, because the renewal of a session needs no password.

47
00:03:08,962 --> 00:03:16,856
Leaked lists are also why, at registration, the platform refuses each password from its list of common and breached passwords.

48
00:03:16,906 --> 00:03:19,352
A few things never happen to a password.

49
00:03:19,402 --> 00:03:23,528
The platform never shows it, returns it, logs it or sends it.

50
00:03:23,578 --> 00:03:26,384
The app masks it on the screen and does not keep it.

51
00:03:26,434 --> 00:03:30,464
And it is not the app PIN, which only opens the app on his phone.

52
00:03:30,514 --> 00:03:32,336
Now a check for you.

53
00:03:32,386 --> 00:03:39,040
Suppose the script types a number that belongs to nobody: does the answer come faster?

54
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:45,952
No: the gateway checks the fixed fake digest, so the answer and its time are the same as for a wrong password.

55
00:03:46,002 --> 00:03:51,856
One row for each number, with a slow, salted Argon2id digest.

56
00:03:51,906 --> 00:03:57,712
The password is checked first, in constant time, even for a number that belongs to nobody.

57
00:03:57,762 --> 00:04:01,768
Wrong guesses lock password login, but they block nothing else.

58
00:04:01,818 --> 00:04:06,064
Aziz has a number, a password and a session, all at level one.

59
00:04:06,114 --> 00:04:09,016
The bank knows his phone, but not yet the person.

60
00:04:09,066 --> 00:04:13,984
Lesson 12 starts on a Saturday, when Aziz wants to send money to his mother.
